Hi Patrick,

This is Xueying again, and I hope this is the last time I post my question for this issue. In my bivariate (x and y) latent growth model with cross-lagged effect, I actually have several x's and y's which could be fit in the model, and I compared the results of before and after mean-centering for each combination of x and y, I found most of the scenario gave the exact same estimates of cross-lagged effects for before and after mean-centering analysis, but there are still a few combinations gave different estimates. And for these different results, I checked the CFI, RMSEA, Chi square and p values, most of them seem not to bad, for example for one combination of x and y, chi2=54.591, df=24, p<0.001, CFI=0.959, RMSEA=0.049. I remember you have said that mean-centering won't change the parameter of cross-lagged effect, so how could I explain the differences? due to small sample size? My sample size is not so big, about 500+, and I also have cluster for the data.

Regards

Xueying

