Yes, Patrick. I am still talking about the mean-centering. x and y, here represent the two manifest variables I used in the bivariate latent growth model, I am sorry for the confusion.

I think I may have made the problem too complicated if I include the mean-centering analysis. In my latent growth model, I am concerned more with the estimates of cross-lagged effects, not the estimates of the latent growth part. I posted this question, mainly because I am afraid readers or reviewers would also have interests on the latent growth parameters, even I could display all the estimates to them, but they are not the whole picture of the latent growth with covariates.

Hopefully you could understand my concern, and thank you very much for your help.

Regards

Xueying

On Sat, 21 Nov 2020 at 18:33, Patrick (Malone Quantitative) <mal...@malonequantitative.com> wrote:

> You're still talking about mean-centering the covariates, right? Not x and y? Because that wouldn't be called for.
>
> If you're only mean-centering covariates, it's possible the issue is that centering works differently for multilevel models (which you didn't mention before).There's an entire literature on the implications of different centering strategies for multilevel data.
>
